  abstract = {The relationship between serum ferritin, brain morphology, and cognition in Alzheimer’s disease (AD) is incompletely characterized. We examined these relationships in a retrospective, cross-sectional cohort of 100 patients with clinically diagnosed probable AD dementia. Multivariate distance matrix regression (MDMR) tested associations between serum ferritin and T1-derived local gray matter profiles of up to 27 values, adjusting for age, sex, and education. Conventional voxelwise partial correlations used the same participants and covariates. An exploratory mediation model related ferritin, regional gray matter, and Chinese Mini-Mental State Examination (CMMS) scores in 95 complete cases. MDMR identified a cluster centered in the left middle cingulate cortex with anterior extension. Higher ferritin was associated with lower regional gray matter values (r = −0.27; nominal p = 0.005), and greater regional gray matter was associated with higher CMMS scores (p = 0.039). The indirect estimate was negative (−0.004; nominal normal-approximation p = 0.034; reported 95% confidence interval, −0.008 to −0.000 at three-decimal precision). Total and direct associations were not statistically significant (p = 0.190 and 0.538). Conventional voxelwise analysis detected no significant voxels at uncorrected p < 0.05. These findings identify cingulate gray matter morphology as a structural correlate of serum ferritin and global cognition in clinically diagnosed probable AD, with the ferritin-as},
